<body>
<sec id="summary">
  <title>Summary</title>
  <p>Quantitative portfolio managers and investors evaluate a strategy
  by simulating a portfolio that implements it and reporting its risk
  and return. Using the target weights of a strategy and the prices of
  its assets, <monospace>qis</monospace> simulates the portfolio’s net
  asset value. It then measures the portfolio against a benchmark and
  renders the result as a factsheet. In addition to the backtesting
  engine, the package provides building blocks for systematic strategies
  and reports, including exponentially weighted covariance, bootstrap
  resampling, risk measures, stress testing, regime-conditional
  statistics, currency hedging, and return unsmoothing.</p>
  <p>Signal generation and portfolio construction remain with the
  researcher. The <monospace>qis</monospace> library reconstructs
  holdings from the target weights and applies explicit accounting and
  reporting conventions.</p>
  <p>The <monospace>qis</monospace> package is the base layer of a
  public stack of quantitative finance packages, whose other members are
  <monospace>optimalportfolios</monospace>,
  <monospace>trendfollowing</monospace>, and
  <monospace>privateassets</monospace>. The package depends on NumPy
  (<xref alt="Harris et al., 2020" rid="ref-numpy" ref-type="bibr">Harris
  et al., 2020</xref>), SciPy
  (<xref alt="Virtanen et al., 2020" rid="ref-scipy" ref-type="bibr">Virtanen
  et al., 2020</xref>), pandas
  (<xref alt="McKinney, 2010" rid="ref-pandas" ref-type="bibr">McKinney,
  2010</xref>), statsmodels
  (<xref alt="Seabold &amp; Perktold, 2010" rid="ref-statsmodels" ref-type="bibr">Seabold
  &amp; Perktold, 2010</xref>), Matplotlib
  (<xref alt="Hunter, 2007" rid="ref-matplotlib" ref-type="bibr">Hunter,
  2007</xref>), seaborn
  (<xref alt="Waskom, 2021" rid="ref-seaborn" ref-type="bibr">Waskom,
  2021</xref>), Numba
  (<xref alt="Lam et al., 2015" rid="ref-numba" ref-type="bibr">Lam et
  al., 2015</xref>), openpyxl, and PyYAML, runs on Python 3.10 to 3.14
  and is released under the MIT licence.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="statement-of-need">
  <title>Statement of need</title>
  <p>The reported performance of a portfolio depends on conventions that
  are rarely stated. Portfolio volatility depends on the return
  frequency, and a Sharpe ratio depends on whether a risk-free rate is
  subtracted and on which rate. A backtested return depends on whether
  holdings drift between rebalancings or are reset every period. Two
  implementations of the same target weights can therefore report
  different performance statistics without either containing an
  error.</p>
  <p>The target users of <monospace>qis</monospace> are researchers and
  portfolio managers who generate weights with their own models. The
  <monospace>qis</monospace> package settles each convention once and
  applies it to every factsheet it generates. The caller states the
  return convention as an argument, and the reporting frequency appears
  on every rendered output. Three Sharpe conventions, with compounded,
  log, and arithmetic numerators, are named and selected explicitly.</p>
</sec>
