  </table-wrap>
  <p>The <monospace>qis</monospace> backtester accepts dated target
  weights and asset prices directly, with optional per-instrument costs
  and carry. For strategies expressed in this form, researchers can
  evaluate allocations generated by their own models without
  implementing a strategy callback or policy object. Target-weight
  support is shared with other packages in
  <xref alt="Table 1" rid="tabU003Afield">Table 1</xref>:
  <monospace>vectorbt</monospace> supports percentage targets, and
  <monospace>zipline-reloaded</monospace> and
  <monospace>backtrader</monospace> provide target-percent order
  methods.</p>
  <p>The contribution of <monospace>qis</monospace> is the integration
  of held-unit accounting, instrument attribution, and comparative
  reporting under explicit conventions. The simulation returns a
  <monospace>PortfolioData</monospace> object carrying net asset value,
  held units, realised weights, realised costs, and instrument profit
  and loss. Factsheets and comparative reports are rendered from it. The
  chart, the summary table, and the factsheet of one strategy therefore
  show one Sharpe ratio under one stated convention. The analytics
  packages <monospace>pyfolio-reloaded</monospace>,
  <monospace>quantstats</monospace>,
  <monospace>empyrical-reloaded</monospace>, and
  <monospace>ffn</monospace> measure a portfolio from its history of
  returns. That history does not record the holdings or the trading
  costs incurred, so these packages cannot show which holding earned or
  lost the money unless holdings are supplied separately. The closest
  package is <monospace>cvxportfolio</monospace>, whose
  <monospace>FixedWeights</monospace> policy trades to supplied target
  weights and holds the positions between them. Its documented backtest
  result reports value, weights, holdings, turnover, and total profit,
  within a framework built around optimisation policies and cost
  models.</p>
  <p>We built a separate library, instead of extending
  <monospace>bt</monospace>, <monospace>ffn</monospace>, or
  <monospace>cvxportfolio</monospace>, because our research packages
  exchange one result object. The <monospace>bt</monospace> package
  already accepts target weights, and <monospace>ffn</monospace> already
  supplies analytics. The <monospace>qis</monospace> package combines
  holdings, costs, attribution, and comparative reports, and its callers
  adopt neither an Algo stack nor a policy framework. The user keeps the
  freedom to generate allocation weights in their own strategy code. The
  trade-off is that <monospace>qis</monospace> ships no optimiser and no
  signal library of its own.</p>
  <p>The integrated analytics include return unsmoothing for
  appraisal-based valuations
  (<xref alt="Geltner, 1993" rid="ref-geltner1993" ref-type="bibr">Geltner,
  1993</xref>;
  <xref alt="Getmansky et al., 2004" rid="ref-getmansky2004" ref-type="bibr">Getmansky
  et al., 2004</xref>), regime-conditional reporting by benchmark return
  quantile
  (<xref alt="Sepp &amp; Dézeraud, 2019" rid="ref-Sepp2019" ref-type="bibr">Sepp
  &amp; Dézeraud, 2019</xref>), and paired resampling of aligned factor
  and residual panels with the stationary bootstrap of Politis &amp;
  Romano
  (<xref alt="1994" rid="ref-politis1994" ref-type="bibr">1994</xref>).
  We do not claim these methods as unique. R’s
  <monospace>PerformanceAnalytics</monospace> implements the first-order
  unsmoothing of Geltner
  (<xref alt="1993" rid="ref-geltner1993" ref-type="bibr">1993</xref>;
  <xref alt="Peterson &amp; Carl, 2026" rid="ref-performanceanalytics" ref-type="bibr">Peterson
  &amp; Carl, 2026</xref>), and the
  <monospace>StationaryBootstrap</monospace> class of
  <monospace>arch</monospace> resamples several aligned arrays with one
  index draw
  (<xref alt="Sheppard &amp; others, 2026" rid="ref-arch" ref-type="bibr">Sheppard
  &amp; others, 2026</xref>). The <monospace>qis</monospace> unsmoothing
  extends the first-order case to any lag order, estimated once for the
  sample or over an exponentially weighted rolling window. Its bootstrap
  resamples returns and recompounds them into price panels.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="software-design">
  <title>Software design</title>
  <p>The backtester is 460 lines, so its accounting can be audited line
  by line. The backtester converts target weights to units at each
  rebalancing, holds those units until the next one, applies costs and
  carry, and returns the result. Holding units lets the realised weights
  drift with prices, whereas multiplying each period’s returns by fixed
  target weights would assume rebalancing in every period. The path
  dependence of held units motivates a date-sequential
  <monospace>numba</monospace> kernel
  (<xref alt="Lam et al., 2015" rid="ref-numba" ref-type="bibr">Lam et
  al., 2015</xref>), and array-based analytics operate on its outputs.
  The backtester suits portfolio research, while order-book events and
  signal generation remain outside it. Market frictions enter instead as
  proportional rebalancing costs, set per instrument and per date.</p>
  <p>The subpackage <monospace>qis.discrete_portfolio</monospace> covers
  strategies that emit orders from a callback on each observation. The
  replay supports full fills at the next observation and converts the
  ledgers into the same <monospace>PortfolioData</monospace> object,
  reconciled against net asset value. Partial fills, cancellations, and
  market impact remain outside this version.</p>
  <p>We build the chart, the summary table, and the factsheet of a
  strategy from one <monospace>PortfolioData</monospace> object,
  constructed from weights and prices. The same object feeds the three
  comparisons of a research pipeline: a strategy against its benchmark,
  a parameter perturbation against its base case, and the sub-strategies
  of one strategy side by side. One reporting layer renders all three
  comparisons under the same conventions for performance evaluation.</p>
  <p>Beyond backtesting, <monospace>qis</monospace> supports analysis of
  currency exposure and factor risk. <monospace>FxRatesData</monospace>
  aligns spot exchange rates and short interest rates to express asset
  returns in a reference currency and evaluate partial currency hedges,
  including carry implied by covered interest parity. Exponentially
  weighted factor models estimate time-varying loadings, while risk
  analytics report portfolio factor exposures and systematic and
  residual risk contributions. Stress-testing functions apply specified
  or conditionally derived factor shocks to current holdings and report
  scenario profit and loss with reconciled factor attribution.</p>
  <p>The public interface is <monospace>qis.__all__</monospace>, which
  holds 480 names. The module <monospace>src/qis/api.py</monospace>
  records that list as a literal, together with a documented core of 166
  symbols in 14 capability groups. The broad namespace preserves
  convenient imports and compatibility for existing consumers, at the
  cost of discoverability. The curated core gives new users a smaller
  entry point without removing any export.</p>
  <p>Tests check exported plots on synthetic panels, the documented
  arguments of the core, local documentation links, and the recorded
  software measurements this paper quotes. Selected offline examples
  execute in the suite, and static checks cover the imports and calls of
  all examples. Plot smoke tests establish that figures draw, while
  layout and readability require visual review. The suite runs without
  network access on a core installation, using a frozen, seeded
  simulator that reproduces the defects of real price panels.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="research-impact-statement">
  <title>Research impact statement</title>
  <p>Within our research stack, <monospace>qis</monospace> carries the
  analytics behind our papers, so others can reproduce their
  calculations with public code. The package supports papers on
  cryptocurrency allocation
  (<xref alt="Sepp, 2023" rid="ref-sepp2023crypto" ref-type="bibr">Sepp,
  2023</xref>) and robust strategic and tactical asset allocation
  (<xref alt="Sepp et al., 2026" rid="ref-sepp2026robust" ref-type="bibr">Sepp
  et al., 2026</xref>). Its bootstrap resampling supports research on
  capital market assumptions using multi-asset tradable factors,
  forthcoming in The Journal of Portfolio Management
  (<xref alt="Sepp et al., 2027" rid="ref-sepp2027matf" ref-type="bibr">Sepp
  et al., 2027</xref>). Its regime-conditional analytics support the
  working paper on trend-following systems
  (<xref alt="Sepp &amp; Lucic, 2026" rid="ref-sepp2026trend" ref-type="bibr">Sepp
  &amp; Lucic, 2026</xref>) and research on portfolio overlays,
  forthcoming in the Journal of Investment Management
  (<xref alt="Sepp &amp; Kastenholz, 2026" rid="ref-sepp2026convexity" ref-type="bibr">Sepp
  &amp; Kastenholz, 2026</xref>). The
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://github.com/ArturSepp/OptimalPortfolios/tree/main/papers/smart_diversification_joim_2026">public
  companion example</ext-link> of the overlay paper demonstrates these
  calculations on synthetic data.</p>
  <p>Two public projects outside our package stack use
  <monospace>qis</monospace>. The
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://github.com/tradingstrategy-ai/optimalportfolios-defi/blob/ea8970bcd7aff61a22078b7baa55600bbb0bfd31/scripts/example.py">example</ext-link>
  in
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://github.com/tradingstrategy-ai/optimalportfolios-defi"><monospace>tradingstrategy-ai/optimalportfolios-defi</monospace></ext-link>
  runs the <monospace>qis</monospace> backtester, strategy factsheet and
  report export on an optimised ETF portfolio. The
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://github.com/luna-007/extra/blob/c71e30753aa47beb3e622a6123a3d9f949228cca/backtest.py">backtesting
  example</ext-link> in
  <ext-link ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://github.com/luna-007/extra"><monospace>luna-007/extra</monospace></ext-link>
  uses <monospace>qis</monospace> to compare leveraged ETF allocations
  with funding costs and to generate a performance factsheet.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="ai-usage-disclosure">
  <title>AI usage disclosure</title>
  <p>From July to October 2026, Anthropic’s Claude (Opus 5 and 5.5,
  Fable 5 and 5.1) and OpenAI Codex (Sol 5.6) assisted with software
  development, tests, documentation, and paper preparation. The author
  made the methodological and architectural decisions, reviewed and
  validated the assisted outputs, and remains responsible for the work.
  Assisted changes pass through the same test suite and continuous
  integration as all other changes. The suite checks recorded software
  measurements against recomputed values or recorded provenance.</p>
</sec>
<sec id="acknowledgements">
  <title>Acknowledgements</title>
  <p>We thank John D. Reynolds for contributions to numerical
  correctness, performance, reporting, and regression test coverage in
  <monospace>qis</monospace>. This work received no specific
  funding.</p>
</sec>
